01. Lifecycle Rules

You can configure something like:
IF object age > 30 days
        ↓
Move to Standard-IA

IF object age > 90 days
        ↓
Move to Glacier Flexible Retrieval

IF object age > 365 days
        ↓
Move to Glacier Deep Archive

You can also configure expiration:

IF object age > 7 years
        ↓
Delete object

This is extremely common in real AWS environments.


S3 Lifecycle

Lifecycle rules automatically transition or delete objects.

Example:

Day 0
 |
 v
S3 Standard
 |
 | 30 days
 v
S3 Standard-IA
 |
 | 90 days
 v
Glacier
 |
 | 365 days
 v
Delete
Real DevOps example

Your application generates:

logs/
├── 2026-09-20.log
├── 2026-09-19.log
├── 2026-08-01.log
└── 2025-01-01.log

You could configure:

0-30 days     → Standard
30-90 days    → Standard-IA
90-365 days   → Glacier
365+ days     → Delete

This reduces storage costs.



02. Versioning
S3 Versioning maintains multiple versions of an object.

Suppose:

app.zip

You upload version 1:

app.zip → v1

Then accidentally overwrite it:

app.zip → v2

With versioning enabled:

app.zip
 ├── Version 1
 └── Version 2

You can recover the older version.


Why Versioning is Important for DevOps

Imagine your CI/CD pipeline uploads:

production/app.zip

A bad deployment overwrites the previous artifact.

Without versioning:

Old artifact ❌

With versioning:

app.zip
├── v1
├── v2
└── v3

You can retrieve an earlier version.



Delete Marker:
When versioning is enabled, deleting an object generally creates a delete marker rather than immediately destroying all previous versions.

Example:

app.zip
├── v1
├── v2
└── Delete Marker

The object appears deleted to normal requests, but previous versions can still exist.




03. S3 Encryption
S3 supports encryption at rest.

Common approaches:

SSE-S3

AWS manages the encryption keys.

Application
     |
     v
S3
     |
     v
AWS-managed encryption
SSE-KMS

Uses AWS KMS.

Application
     |
     v
S3
     |
     v
AWS KMS

Useful when you need more control over:

Key policies
Auditing
Permissions
Key rotation
SSE-C

Customer provides the encryption key with the request.

Less commonly used in typical AWS DevOps environments.


Encryption in Transit:
Use HTTPS/TLS when communicating with S3.

Application
     |
   HTTPS
     |
     v
    S3

You generally want to prevent insecure HTTP access using an S3 bucket policy.



04. IAM + S3
This is extremely important.

Suppose an EC2 server needs to upload files to S3.

Don't put:

AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY

inside your application code.

Instead:

EC2
 |
 | IAM Role
 |
 v
S3

Example policy:

{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Action": [
    "s3:GetObject",
    "s3:PutObject"
  ],
  "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::my-app-bucket/*"
}

This follows the least privilege principle better than giving the instance broad S3 permissions.



05. IAM User vs IAM Role

Bad approach:

EC2
 |
 +-- AWS Access Key
 +-- AWS Secret Key
 |
 v
S3

Better:

EC2
 |
 +-- IAM Role
 |
 v
S3

For AWS services, prefer IAM roles rather than long-lived access keys whenever possible.



06. Bucket Policy

A bucket policy is a resource-based policy attached to an S3 bucket.

Example:

Bucket
  |
  +--- Bucket Policy
           |
           +--- Who can access?
           +--- What actions?
           +--- Which objects?

You can use bucket policies to control access from:

IAM principals
AWS services
Specific conditions
Specific VPC endpoints, where applicable
